.TH sed 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
sed \- stream editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sed
[\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-g\fR] \fIscript\fR [\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
.B sed
[\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-g\fR] [\fB\-e\fR \fIscript\fR] ... [\fB\-f\fR \fIscriptfile\fR] ... [\fIfile\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sed
copies each
.I file
to standard output, applying the editing commands in
.I script
to every line as it passes.  With no
.IR file ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
Each line is read into the
.IR "pattern space" ,
the commands are applied to it in order, and unless
.B \-n
was given the pattern space is printed and the cycle begins again.  A
second buffer, the
.IR "hold space" ,
survives between cycles and is reached with
.BR g ,
.BR G ,
.BR h ,
.B H
and
.BR x .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " script"
Add
.I script
to the commands.  May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-f " scriptfile"
Add the contents of
.I scriptfile
to the commands.  May be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-g
Treat every
.B s
command as if it had the
.B g
flag.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not print the pattern space at the end of each cycle.  Only
.BR p ,
.B P
and
.B w
produce output.
.PP
If no
.B \-e
and no
.B \-f
is given, the first argument is the script.
.SH ADDRESSES
A command may be preceded by no address (every line), one address (lines
that match), or two separated by a comma (from the first match to the
next line matching the second).
.TP
.I n
Line number
.IR n .
.TP
.B $
The last line of the last input file.
.TP
.BI / re /
Lines matching the regular expression
.IR re .
An empty
.B //
reuses the last expression.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B a\\
Append the text on the following lines after the current line.
.TP
.BI b " label"
Branch to
.IR label ,
or to the end of the script if none is given.
.TP
.B c\\
Replace the current line with the text on the following lines.
.TP
.B d
Delete the pattern space and start the next cycle.
.TP
.B D
Delete up to the first newline in the pattern space and restart the
script without reading a new line.
.TP
.B g
Copy the hold space over the pattern space.
.TP
.B G
Append a newline and the hold space to the pattern space.
.TP
.B h
Copy the pattern space over the hold space.
.TP
.B H
Append a newline and the pattern space to the hold space.
.TP
.B i\\
Insert the text on the following lines before the current line.
.TP
.B l
Print the pattern space with non-printing characters shown as escapes.
.TP
.B n
Print the pattern space (unless
.BR \-n )
and read the next line into it.
.TP
.B N
Append a newline and the next input line to the pattern space.
.TP
.B p
Print the pattern space.
.TP
.B P
Print up to the first newline of the pattern space.
.TP
.B q
Print the pattern space unless
.BR \-n ,
and exit.
.TP
.BI r " file"
Read
.I file
and copy it out after the current line.
.TP
.BI s/ re / replacement /[flags]
Substitute.  See below.
.TP
.BI t " label"
Branch to
.I label
if any
.B s
has succeeded since the last input line or
.BR t .
.TP
.BI T " label"
Branch to
.I label
if no
.B s
has succeeded.
.TP
.BI w " file"
Append the pattern space to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI W " file"
Append the first line of the pattern space to
.IR file .
.TP
.B x
Exchange the pattern and hold spaces.
.TP
.BI y/ from / to /
Transliterate the characters of
.I from
into those of
.IR to .
.TP
.B =
Print the current line number.
.TP
.BI : label
Define a branch target.
.TP
.B { }
Group commands so that one address governs them all.
.TP
.B #
Comment to end of line.
.PP
Commands may be separated by newlines or by semicolons.
.SH THE s COMMAND
Any character may be the delimiter, not just
.BR / .
In the replacement,
.B &
stands for the whole matched text, and
.BR \\1 " to " \\9
for the text matched by the corresponding
.BR \\( ... \\)
group.
.PP
The flags are:
.TP
.B g
Replace every occurrence on the line, not just the first.
.TP
.B p
Print the pattern space if a replacement was made.
.TP
.B P
Print the first line of it.
.TP
.BI w " file"
Write the pattern space to
.I file
if a replacement was made.
.SH REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
Basic regular expressions:
.BR . ,
.BR * ,
.BR [ ... ] ,
.BR [^ ... ] ,
.B ^
and
.B $
at the ends,
.BR \\( ... \\)
for grouping, and
.BR \\1 " to " \\9
for what those groups matched.
.PP
There are no intervals:
.B \\{2,5\\}
is not understood, and is
.I silently treated as literal text
rather than reported.  There is no
.B \\+
and no
.BR \\? ,
and no extended regular expressions.
.SH NOTES
This is the MINIX sed by Eric S. Raymond.  Beside the missing regular
expression operators above, the differences from GNU sed that catch
people out are:
.TP
.B \(bu
The
.B s
command takes no occurrence number.
.B s/a/X/2
is rejected with a complaint about trailing garbage rather than
replacing the second match.
.TP
.B \(bu
There is no
.B \-i
in-place edit, no
.BR \-r ,
no
.BR \-E ,
and no
.B I
case-insensitive flag.
.TP
.B \(bu
.B \-g
is a FUZIX and MINIX extension, not a GNU one.
.SH LIMITS
1000 characters to a line, and the same to the hold space; 256 compiled
commands; 256 numeric addresses; 10000 characters of text pool for the
expressions and replacements; 10
.B w
files; 20 levels of braces; 50 labels.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 normally, 2 for a bad script or an unreadable file.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# sed s/red/green/g report.txt
# sed -n /ERROR/p log
# sed -n 10,20p file
# sed -e /^#/d -e /^$/d config
# ls | sed s/.bas$/.bak/
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR awk (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR ed (1),
.BR tr (1)
